Date: Sun, 18 Apr 93 13:35:51 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Crypto Resources--Information, Where to Look, etc.

Several Extropians have requested more information on crypto, on RSA, on
digital cash, and so forth. Their sudden interest is no surprise, of
course. Some are newcomers to the list, others may have skipped the
discussion last time it went around.

Here are some miscellaneous sources of info:

* Read "sci.crypt" first. It contains the usual smattering of specialized
articles, newbie requests ("Is it true DES has a trapdoor?" is a perennial
favorite), and FAQs. Lance Dettweiler, one of our Cypherpunks, has written
an excellent and long series of FAQs on "Privacy on the Internet." Also,
several Extropians are well-represented in the debates, including Marc
Ringuette, Hal Finney, Perry Metzger, and others. The "A Trial Balloon to
Ban Encryption?" posting I made last fall on the Dorothy Denning comments
generated over 1000 followups. In retrospect, this key registration "trial
balloon" was already well along.

* Hal Finney wrote a nice piece on "Digital Money" in the latest (#10)
"Extropy." Anyone who wants to critique digital cash (no slam against
Stanton M. :-}) might first want to read this article to see what it's
really all about.

* Ray Cromwell just posted a good summary of the RSA algorithm. Previously,
many of us have posted similar articles on RSA, the Dining Cryptographer's
Protocol, digital money, "Libertaria in Cyberspace," etc. (I joined the
list last July and after waiting several weeks to "get the lay of the
land," began posting on crypto and similar themes...I just don't have the
energy to rewrite these essays, and simply reposting them destroys the
context in which they were written.)

* various articles, including my "Crypto Anarchist Manifesto" and "Glossary
of Crypto Terms" (co-authored with Eric Hughes), are contained in the
/pub/cypherpunks (or similar) directory at "soda.berkeley.edu". You can ftp
to this site and transfer the files. (These have also been posted one or
more times to this list, perhaps you have them already in your own mail
archives.)

* the Cypherpunks mailing list has many Extropians on it (a dozen or more,
I'd guess). It was started last fall by Eric Hughes and me as a forum for
discussing and building crypto tools and systems. About 200 on the mailing
list, last I heard. You can join be sending a request to
"cypherpunks-request@toad.com" and saying the secret word (just a joke,
from the ghost of Klaus!).

* the latest issue of "Wired" has a cover story on crypto and
cypherpunks...I still haven't seen it, but folks who have are saying it's
pretty well done. Lots of other magazines are also planning coverage of
this issue...the Wiretap Chip may spur on even more coverage. And your
local university library may have the issues of "Computer" and
"Communications of the ACM" that have carried articles on these issues.

* the Extropians list has debated these topics many, many times. I suggest
that this time around we not debate the basics of crypto. Please read the
articles, the FAQs, and consult the various books on crypto if you are
really interested.

Well, these are a few places to look for info.

-Tim May

