Date: Sun, 18 Apr 93 21:03:20 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Robin Hanson still suggests that in making contact with aliens we should
send holes to quasars or other Schelling points.  I prefer Schelling
points based on the geometry defined by our previous interactions.

On reflection I agree that you're unlikely to be able to pinpoint the
alien hole's destruction site.  The debris from their hole would be lost
in the immensity of space.  But my solution of heading towards the
destruction site (of our own hole only) still seems a more natural
Schelling point, to me, than any other.  

But, what the heck, no reason why we can't do both!  Send some holes off
to the nearest quasar and some to the destruction site.

> Going slower the next time doesn't solve the problem that the holes
> threaten to form a causal loop if they cross each other.

If each side decelerates with respect to the co-moving frame then the
threat of new causal loop forming is reduced.  Travelling more slowly
means that you can trail out holes more densely behind you this time. 
When you lose your lead hole you can do a binary chop on the search
volume and repeat the procedure.

> And going slower takes up empire time.

But you'd have this disadvantage at a quasar as well.  Empire time for
a stationary hole, at the quasar, is the same as co-moving time.  First
hole to get to the quasar has to kill time until the second one shows
up, which since you don't know the trip time of the second hole to the
quasar, could be *millions* of years in empire time.....  

Searching for each other by firing test holes at each other and binary
chopping the resultant search volume going is going to be a lot quicker,
in empire time, than hanging about waiting for contact.  You would trade
off dropping off of slower marker holes with the time-dilated probing
of the relativistic test holes.

> Imagine that we sent out holes as soon as we were able and they
> collided 25 empire years later.  Would we *want* to try hard to meet
> up with the aliens soon after that?

I agree that it would be best to postpone contact until we'd plateaued
out.  By definition this would not be until some time after we're
capable of sending out hole to explore the universe.  Sounds like one
of those hard problems.  Without central control how could such
restraint be exercised?  In a free-market lead economy there'd always
be some (witness SETI, Pioneer-10 and the Voyager probes) who would make
contact as soon as possible and hang the consequences.  With Tipler
space probes we were insulated against the effects meeting superior
aliens by the trip travel times.  With holes they can get at us too
easily for comfort.

Once we've plateaued out then holes could be rigged against seizure
without to much trouble.  I'm worried about the period before that.

> Brash civilizations that choose to meet up as soon as they are able
> might be easy prey for those that wait until their abilities have
> reached more of a plateau.

The meek shall inherit the universe?  :-) & :-(

> Robin Hanson

