Date: Thu, 15 Apr 93 07:07:47 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

> Hal Finney writes:
>> It seems like something similar would happen with the wormholes as
>> they got into causal loops. [...]  An infinite flux would appear 
>> just as it became paradoxical.
> 
> But isn't there a logical problem here? Aren't you supposing a
> mechanism that prevents temporal loops that actually relies on
> temporal loops to work?

> Elias Israel

I'll stick my neck out and guess that the mechanisms that prevent time
travel come into play just before it becomes possible for a ray of light
to form a closed time-like loop (CTL) through the putative time machine. 
The operation of a time machine represents a singular solution.  The
quantum fluctuation effects of vacuum polarisation screen these acausal
infinities from us and only permit finite, causal solutions.

Virtual particles (they appear on Feynman diagrams as exchange
particles) can exert an influence between points that are space-like
separated (ie points in space time that cannot be connected by a ray of
light).  It doesn't seem unreasonable to me to suppose that these
critters can start having an effect just *before* the time machine
permits non-virtual CTLs.

In the case of a Tipler neutronium cylinder, for example, to turn it
into a time machine a certain angular momentum has to be built up.  Just
before the critical angular momentum is reached the vacuum polarises
around the cylinder.  Virtual particles fly around cylinder, forming
closed space-like loops.  The angular momentum of these particles oppose
the angular momentum of the neutronium.  So you'd have to spin the
cylinder faster....  An analogy might be with back EMF in motor,
opposing the applied EMF.  In this case the back EMF effect is so strong
the motor is overwhelmed and never reaches critical.

I suspect that this opposing effect is generic to all time machines. 
Any attempt to construct a time machine will be confounded by space-like
vacuum polarisation effects.

