Edgar Swank says:

> I'm surprised to hear that the chances of FTL are theoretically
> improved at the same time that those for time travel are disimproved.

I share your surprise.  When I first came across the Chronology
Protection Conjecture I dismissed it as fanciful, wishful thinking. 
Having reconciled time travel with physics, to my satisfaction, I
thought CPC was just a mental defence mechanism for the irreconciled.

> I seem to recall hearing that, according to relativity(?), FTL travel
> is -equivalent- to time travel; if you can do either you can do both.

You recall correctly, relativity does imply the equivalence of FTL and
time travel.  Visser has overturned this relativistic equivalence by
including quantum field theory in his calculations.  Visser provides
evidence that:
      relativity ==> (FTL <==> time travel)
but that
      quantum field theory ==> (FTL =/=> time travel)
Quantum field theory includes relativity and so its conclusions
supersede those of classical relativity.

However, time travel ==> FTL, as far as I know.  Not that that implies
time travel (or FTL) is possible.  Just that FTL travel is more likely
than time travel.

Visser says that the prospects for FTL look brighter as time travel has
faded.  This is because he belongs to the camp that rejects time travel. 
If FTL implied time travel then he would conclude that FTL is
impossible.  Can't agree with his motivation but his conclusions seem
unimpeachable - its been published in reputable journal and no one's
rubbished it yet.

Also, my thanks to Tim May for the Cosmic Wormholes reference.  I've not
got hold of a copy yet, but I shall carry on looking.

