Well, I am unconcerned with the opinions of one poster.  But 2 things I have
noted here tend to disturb me.  No make that 3.

1) the "Extropianly Correct" silliness that was just lambasted, which mani-
fests not just in hostility to newbies, but in a lot of general flammage.
Those with this viewpoint would do well to take into consideration that 
simply because a person is new to this list does NOT mean they are new
to Extropy, and further more not all extropians are Extropians, just as
not all libertarians are members of the Libertarian party.  Many of us are
interested in specific issues, that incidentally are much discussed on this
list, and are not interested in others.  We may become interested in an issue,
say time travel, by encountering it's mention here.  Condescending to such 
people because they are genuinely curious about an issue, and telling them
"RTFM" is not only generally dishelpful, but counterproductive.  Elitism
may have its uses, but swelling the ranks of the extropians is not one of 
them.  And lastly on this subject, many of us have an extreme aversion to 
"-isms" of any sort, including this one.  I have often felt somewhat put-upon
to "be Extropian", when I literally cannot.  I am an individual, period.

2) I have also noted a certain haughty disdain for things that one does not
choose to put any faith in.  Rather than serious discussion, the reaction 
tends to be "oh you are just a clueless clod who does not possess the 
Extropian Nature", etc.  Certainly everyone has pet theories, but that is
no reason to be illogical about it.  Granted I exaggerate, and no one really
puts it quite that bluntly, but it is oozing out from between the lines in
several messages I have seen on the list so far.  Certainly no one here wants
their mail box full of "I think Velikovsky was right!", much less "what does
'public key' mean?"  But reasonable debates, and short simple answers to ba-
sic questions, are not so hard to muster are they?  As for an example of 
haughtiness, I point you to the post that, in essence, told the recipient "Ah,
now you have learned to think.  Go read some Aristotle."  I don't think I have
ever heard such conceit in all my life, unless that was meant jokingly.

3) A smaller gripe, and one I have with a lot of intellectual-oriented con-
ferences: Why be so quick to correct every typo and questionable statement?
My mailbox overfloweth with people correcting eachother on points of no
consequence.  If the matter is important, then go for it.  But is letting 
things slide Extropianly Incorrect?  I don't mind being corrected if it is an
provable mistake, but I see no point in mailing, either to the list or in
private, "corrections" that are just as much a matter of conjecture, opinion,
or questionable source as the original post.

That's all.  Besides those 3 points, I like this list, and am glad I am
receiving it.  I was criticized, rightly, for overposting, and apologize for
that, but I do intend to be here for a while and to contribute what I can.
Thanks. 
