From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Big Brother: Easy to Break

Cal Feynman writes:
>I was talking about the 56-bit keys used for DES.  80 bits is long
>enough to preclude reasonable solution times for simple exhausive
>search.  Here's how I estimate this.  Assume that a Clipper uses an
>eight-byte block, and can handle 16Kbytes/sec.  That means a single
>chip decryption engine can test 2000 keys/sec.  If Clipper costs $10,
>and the NSA spends $10^8 on an exhausive cracking machine, they can
>test 2x10^11 keys/sec.  Unfortunately, testing all 2^80=10^24 keys
>will take 5x10^12 sec, which is hundreds of years.  

OK, but suppose that they split the keys up as follows. Give half to
some agency or organization that is generally trusted by the public
(you could even go so far as to give them to the ACLU) and then give
the other have to someone more pliable (say, Justice, FBI, or NSA).

If you can only get half of the 80-bit key, you're left with a 40-bit
key that you have to crack, no? Is there a way to arrange this so that
having half of the key does not open the encrypted text to brute-force
attack? I appears to me to be an integral part of this plan. Else, why
bother with this 40+40=80-bit key escrow nonsense? The numbers can't
be accidental.

I don't know much about crypto, but the more I learn and the more I
think about the clipper, the more this big brother proposal seems
like a film of lies.

